.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tatr
is a task tracker utility tooling. It enables the end-user to track the up-and-coming tasks to do. The tooling can create
.B new
tasks,
.B list
them using filters,
.B find
a task by its
.IR id .
The user can also
.B edit
a task also by its
.IR id ,
.B close
it,
.B delete
or
.B remove
it, or multiple at once.
.P
This tool is a replacement for the issue tracker that github has. Basically a, \&"Mom can we have Github's\&
.IR "issue tracker" ?
No, we have
.I "issue tracker"
\&at home.\|"\&
.P
Although, to note that this tool was original created by [@Tsoding](https://github.com/Tsoding).
.br
This is only my implementation of it, with added features, and adjusted to my liking.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B tatr help
.br
Shows the help message similar to this one.
.TP
.BI tatr\ version
.br
Prints the version of the utility, the current git-hash baked into the executable when it was last compiled.
.TP
.BI tatr\ init \ [y]
.br
Initialises the
.I cwd
with a
.RI \|"tasks/"\|
directory, it does not yet exists. Adding "y" as an option forces the creation of the directory.
.TP
.BI tatr\ find \ <task-huid>
.br
Displays the information of a task, provided by its id.
.TP
.BI tatr\ cat \ <task-huid>
.br
Prints to the terminal the information of a task, provided by its id.
.TP
.B tatr summary
.br
Displays general information about the tasks. Statistics relative to the tags and amount of tasks.
.TP
.BI tatr\ edit \ <task-huid>
.br
Opens in your
.I $EDITOR
of choice the task provided by its id. Defaults to vim.
.TP
.BI tatr\ close \ <task-huid> \ \&.\|.\|.\&
.br
Closes the task or tasks provided by their id.
.TP
.BI tatr\ reopen \ <task-huid> \ \&.\|.\|.\&
.br
Reopens the task or tasks provided by their id.
.TP
.BI tatr\ rm \ [\|-last\ <int>\|]\ <task-huid> \ \&.\|.\|.\&
.br
Deletes the task or tasks provided by their id. Using
.BI tatr \ del
also works the same.
.br
Using the
.B -last
flag let's you delete the n most recent task created.
.TP
.B tatr\ ls\ [\|ls\ options\|]\ [\|"<name>"\|]
.br
Displays every task answering to the query given by
.IR ls\ options \&.\&
.br
A query is formed of
.I \&.<tag>\&
and keywords. Keywords include
.BR and ,
.BR or ,
and
.BR not .
.br
Amongst tags, some are pre-defined by the tool, including:
.BR .all ,
.BR .OPEN ,
.BR .CLOSED ,
.BR .TAGGED ,
and
.BR .UNTAGGED .
.br
To note, an empty query defaults to
.BR .OPEN .
Adding to it result in
.BI .OPEN\ and \ <query> ,
unless otherwise specified
.BR .all ,
or
.BR .CLOSED \ tags.
.br
Moreover, filtering by
.I name
is mutually exclusive to filtering by
.IR tags .
.TP
.BI tatr\ new\ \-t \ <tag>,\&.\|.\|.\& \ \-p \ <int>\ "title"
Creates a new task of title
.IR "title" .
.br
.I new options
allow you to add comma seperated tags, and to define the task's priority as it's being created.
.TP
.B tatr\ overwrite\ [\|overwrite\ options\|] \ <task-huid [...] | query>
.br
Overwrites task(s)'s tag, title, priority and status all at once.
.br
The
.IR overwrite\ options
are similar to the
.B tatr new
options. They allow you to directly modify a task's properties from the CLI.
.br
.BI \-p \ <int>
allows you to change the task's priority
.br
.BI \-t \ <tag>,\&.\|.\|.\&
allows you to overwrite the task's tags
.br
.BI \-t \ +<tag>,-<tag>\&.\|.\|.\&
allows you to add or remove tags from the task's tags. Which is exclusive to overwriting the tags whole.
.br
.BI \-s \ O[\|pen\|]
|
.I C[\|losed\|]
changes the task's status to opened or closed
.br
.BI \-r \ "title"
allows you to rename the task. Be careful as this can be used with queries to mass rename to the same task name.
.SH Examples
.TP
tatr new -t feature,debug "this is a feature to be added for debug purposed" -p 42
.br
Creates a task named "this is a feature to be added for debug purposed" of priority 42 with the tags feature and debug.
.TP
tatr ls .CLOSED and .feature
.br
Lists closed tasks having the feature tag
.TP
tatr ls "this debug f"
.br
Lists all of the tasks featuring to an extent "this debug f" in their task name
.TP
tatr ls .all and .debug
.br
Lists all opened and closed tasks having the debug tag
.TP
tatr ls .debug or .feature
.br
Lists all opened tasks having the debug or feature tags
.TP
tatr cat 20260727-133742
.br
Prints to the stdout the details of task 20260727-133742
.TP
tatr close 20260727-133742 20260420-012345
.br
Closes both tasks
.SH NOTES
.TP
As of 2026-07-03, the filtering system still lacks enclosing with parenthesis and name filtering.
.TP
As of 2026-07-04, the filtering system now fully supports enclosing with parenthesis
.TP
As of 2026-07-06, you can now either filter by name or by tags
.TP
As of 2026-08-24, query language now works using a proper lexer, parser and even an ast tree.
.TP
As of 2026-09-14, you can permissively modify tasks' properties from the command line using the `overwrite` option
